Wc.jpeg","profile":"","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:2401660","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":4909193,"name":"Anastasios Skalkos"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["DJ Taso","Taso Teklife"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/2401660-Taso","type":"artist","urls":["https://www.facebook.com/theofficialtaso","http://www.soundcloud.com/taso","http://www.tasomusic.com","http://www.teklife57.com","http://www.twitter.com/tasoteklife"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/42627/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"42627","image":"https://i.discogs.com/rADpH34oK2Sm7BdzFPoyHE9fsYt_nIn88QrezU3NDKA/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:484/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTQyNjI3/LTE2NDg1NjQ5Mzkt/MTk2Mi5qcGVn.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/zjR1K9pLexic8yWR11EKc5DgRVisGyvp0HPPZgNnGlY/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTQyNjI3/LTE2NDg1NjQ5Mzkt/MTk2Mi5qcGVn.jpeg","profile":"American singer-songwriter, rapper, recording artist, musician, producer, and actress born on May 26th, 1975 in East Orange, NJ. Early in her career, she established her reputation as a member of the [a=Fugees]. In 1998, she launched her solo career with the release of the commercially successful and critically acclaimed album, [m=57279]. The recording earned Hill five Grammy Awards, including the coveted album of the year and best new artist awards.\r\nFollowing the success of her debut album, Hill largely dropped out of public view, in part due to her displeasure with fame and the music industry. After a four-year hiatus, she released [m=57295], a live recording of \"deeply personal songs\" performed mostly solo with an acoustic guitar. Today, she avoids publicity, and is the mother of five children with [a=Rohan Marley], the fourth son of reggae musician [a=Bob Marley].\r\n","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:42627","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":585117,"name":"L-Boogie"}],"groups":[{"active":true,"id":18231,"name":"Fugees"},{"active":true,"id":706445,"name":"The Tranzlator Crew"},{"active":true,"id":885957,"name":"The St. Francis Choir"},{"active":true,"id":1835216,"name":"Guantanamo Camp"}],"members":null,"namevariations":["Fugees (Lauryn Hill)","Hill","L Hill","L. H.","L. Hill","L. Mohagany","L.Hill","Laneyn Hill","Laureen Hill","Lauren","Lauren Hil","Lauren Hill","Laurin Hill","Laurun Hill","Lauryen Hill","Lauryl Hill","Lauryn","Lauryn N Hill","Lauryn N. Hill","Lauryn, Voc.","Lauyn","Lauyrn","Lawryn Hill","Layryn Hill","Ms Lauryn Hill","Ms ローリン・ヒル","Ms. Lauren Hill","Ms. Lauryn Hill","S. Michel","ローリン・ヒル","羅倫希爾","蘿倫希爾"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/42627-Lauryn-Hill","type":"artist","urls":["http://www.lauryn-hill.com","https://www.facebook.com/mslaurynhill/","http://www.myspace.com/laurynhill","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lauryn_Hill","https://www.instagram.com/mslaurynhill/","https://x.com/MsLaurynHill","https://www.threads.com/@mslaurynhill"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/70828/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"70828","image":"https://i.discogs.com/tD9kMI9GK7WGiFo2bKOQ6WNZIqzVmBBqNsE8Jc57byA/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTcwODI4/LTEyMzY2MTc0ODgu/anBlZw.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/pHb5FbThjF-W_dPx4E3JH1kBaWmDDi8usyn_AH5JBi4/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTcwODI4/LTEyMzY2MTc0ODgu/anBlZw.jpeg","profile":"King Crimson were an English-based progressive rock band formed in 1968. One of the pioneers of the progressive rock genre. The first official rehearsal of the band was on January 13, 1969. The first line-up comprised guitarist [a=Robert Fripp], lyricist and lighting man [a=Peter Sinfield] (who “invented” the name of the band), composer and multi-instrumentalist [a=Ian McDonald], bassist and vocalist [a=Greg Lake], and drummer [a=Michael Giles]. They toured extensively and released the album [m=406634], a seminal piece of late ’60s music. Shortly afterwards, the band split to reform again suffering continuous personnel changes for a period of two and a half years (early 1970-mid 1972), releasing three more studio albums and one recorded live, with Robert Fripp as the only remaining member.\r\n\r\nThe new King Crimson that evolved in July 1972 (featuring ex-[a=Yes] drummer [a=Bill Bruford], ex-[a=Family (6)] and later [a=Asia (2)] bassist/vocalist [a=John Wetton] and other more transitory members) marked a turn toward a heavier progressive sound, with experimental and fusion overtones, climaxing in unique semi-improvisatory live performances. This core line-up carried on until mid-1974, when Fripp broke up the band (as he thought) for good.\r\n\r\nIn mid-1981, after a full 7 years, a newly-formed band including Robert Fripp, with [a=Adrian Belew] on vocals and guitar, [a=Tony Levin] on bass and Chapman Stick, and Bill Bruford on acoustic and electronic drums, changed its name from [url=http://www.discogs.com/artist/Discipline+(6)]Discipline[/url] to King Crimson. This line-up remained intact until summer 1984, releasing three studio albums.\r\n\r\nTen years later (May 1994), King Crimson started rehearsing again, this time as a \"double trio\" including [a=Pat Mastelotto] on drums and percussion and [a=Trey Gunn] on Stick in addition to the 1980s line-up; it released two full albums and a handful of EPs through Fripp's own [l=Discipline Global Mobile] label. A process of “fractalization” led to the creation of multiple spin-off groups containing three or four King Crimson members, dubbed \"ProjeKcts\" [url=http://www.discogs.com/artist/ProjeKct+One]One[/url], [url=http://www.discogs.com/artist/Projekct+Two]Two[/url], [url=http://www.discogs.com/artist/ProjeKct+Three]Three[/url], [url=http://www.discogs.com/artist/Projekct+Four]Four[/url], and [url=http://www.discogs.com/artist/Projekct+X]X[/url], which released live and studio sessions through DGM. Having regained the rights to the full King Crimson catalogue, DGM put out numerous other recordings from all periods of the band's existence, including \"King Crimson Collectors' Club\" bi-monthly releases available only to members of the label's website. In the hands of DGM, the King Crimson back catalogue (with most late additions available only on FLAC/MP3 format rather than on CD) grew enormously, making it one of the biggest for any rock group ever.\r\n\r\nThe next reincarnation of the band, jokingly dubbed the \"double duo\", omitted Bill Bruford and Tony Levin. Its first complete studio album was “The ConstruKction of Light” (May 2000), and the line-up carried on until the end of 2003. Since then, Tony Levin rejoined the group and Trey Gunn departed. In 2008, with the addition of [a=Gavin Harrison] the new King Crimson began and performed a 40th Anniversary Tour.\r\n\r\nRobert Fripp stated in an August 2012 interview that he had retired from the music business, but 2014 found a new King Crimson line-up touring, including Fripp (guitar), Mel Collins (saxophones, flute), Tony Levin (basses, stick), Pat Mastelotto (drums, percussion), Gavin Harrison (drums, percussion), [a=Jakko M. Jakszyk] (vocals, guitar, flute), and [a=William Rieflin] (drums, percussion, synthesizer). In 2016 [a=Jeremy Stacey] (drums, keyboards) joined as an eighth member making the group a \"double quartet\".\r\n\r\nThe band toured North America and then Japan in 2021. Levin and Jakszyk had earlier said that the North America tour was probably the band's last there.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:70828","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":521561,"name":"ProjeKct X"},{"id":578583,"name":"Discipline (6)"},{"id":1364824,"name":"81 To 99"}],"groups":null,"members":[{"active":true,"id":10355,"name":"Robert Fripp"},{"active":true,"id":18160,"name":"Mel Collins"},{"active":false,"id":55902,"name":"Adrian Belew"},{"active":false,"id":129858,"name":"Bill Bruford"},{"active":false,"id":132196,"name":"William Rieflin"},{"active":false,"id":215685,"name":"David Cross"},{"active":false,"id":235032,"name":"Keith Tippett"},{"active":false,"id":252061,"name":"John Wetton"},{"active":false,"id":252062,"name":"Jamie Muir"},{"active":false,"id":253141,"name":"Gordon Haskell"},{"active":false,"id":253142,"name":"Robin Miller"},{"active":false,"id":253143,"name":"Andy McCulloch"},{"active":false,"id":253144,"name":"Peter Sinfield"},{"active":true,"id":256225,"name":"Tony Levin"},{"active":false,"id":259119,"name":"Ian McDonald"},{"active":false,"id":259120,"name":"Greg Lake"},{"active":false,"id":259121,"name":"Michael Giles"},{"active":false,"id":270315,"name":"Trey Gunn"},{"active":true,"id":285583,"name":"Pat Mastelotto"},{"active":false,"id":299473,"name":"Mark Charig"},{"active":true,"id":307109,"name":"Jeremy Stacey"},{"active":false,"id":337084,"name":"Boz Burrell"},{"active":false,"id":337085,"name":"Ian Wallace"},{"active":true,"id":386961,"name":"Gavin Harrison"},{"active":true,"id":431081,"name":"Jakko M. Jakszyk"},{"active":false,"id":1716879,"name":"Raymond Burrell"},{"active":false,"id":7286284,"name":"Chris Gibson (10)"}],"namevariations":["Crim","Crimson","K. Crimson","King Crimson 1974","King Krimson","キング・クリムゾン","킹크림슨"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/70828-King-Crimson","type":"artist","urls":["https://www.dgmlive.com/","https://www.facebook.com/kingcrimsonofficial/","https://x.com/dgmhq","https://www.Instagram.com/dgmlive/","https://www.youtube.com/user/DGMLiveHQ","https://www.youtube.com/@kingcrimson","https://www.elephant-talk.com/wiki/ETWiki_Home","https://www.progarchives.com/artist.asp?id=191","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_Crimson","https://www.imdb.com/name/nm1072743/","https://www.allmusic.com/artist/king-crimson-mn0000076057"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/820733/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"820733","image":"https://i.discogs.com/Tsn1GOYImf5AlQ6Edw99QgaT4MIYx0pxknCvl4lya0U/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:500/w:500/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTgyMDc